  "body": "   Every covariant representable preserves small limits. The contravariant functor sends colimit cocones to limit cones of sets indexed by the opposite category.  Every fully faithful functor reflects limits and colimits. An equivalence preserves and reflects both, and existence of a limit or colimit for a given diagram is invariant under applying an equivalence.     The first claims are precisely the compatible-family bijections in . For reflection, let be fully faithful and suppose the image of is limiting. A cone with vertex gives a unique map with . Fullness lifts to ; faithfulness gives . Any competing factorization has the same image by uniqueness in , and is equal to by faithfulness.  If is also essentially surjective, start with a limiting cone in and a cone over its image with arbitrary vertex . Choose an isomorphism . Precomposing its legs with and using full faithfulness yields a cone in with vertex . Its unique factorization, followed by on the source after applying , gives a factorization from . The same procedure proves uniqueness. Thus an equivalence preserves limits as well as reflecting them.  A quasi-inverse is itself an equivalence, so it too preserves limits. If admits a limit, apply the quasi-inverse and then the natural isomorphism between the resulting diagram and to obtain a limit of . This proves the assertion about existence. All colimit statements follow by reversing arrows.   "
