  "body": " Filtered colimit   Let be small and filtered, and . For and , set if there exist arrows , with . Then this is an equivalence relation on the tagged disjoint union, and Write , or when unambiguous, for the corresponding class. For two elements at the same stage, the sharper criterion is     Identities prove reflexivity, and symmetry follows by exchanging the two arrows. For transitivity, suppose , , and , with witnesses so that and . Choose common-target arrows and . The composites may be different. Choose equalizing them. Then The arrows and witness .  Every generating colimit identification satisfies this relation, using and the identity of its target. Conversely, a common-stage equality relates both elements to the same image through generating identifications. Thus the equivalence relation is precisely the colimit relation from the set construction in . The quotient belongs to because the index and all values are small.  For the sharper assertion, the common-stage criterion initially gives possibly different arrows with . Equalize by and put . Applying gives . The converse uses as both common-stage arrows.   "
