  "title": "Tests that detect and tests that reconstruct.",
  "body": " Tests that detect and tests that reconstruct   The functor selecting a singleton is dense: its nerve is naturally the identity functor on sets, since . Its canonical presentation writes a set as the coproduct of its singleton elements.  Let be the small category of finite ordinals and all functions between them. Its inclusion in is dense. A natural transformation between its restricted nerves is determined on the singleton by a function . For , naturality with every point forces its image to be ; for there is only one possible function. Hence it is postcomposition by , proving fullness, and the singleton also proves faithfulness.  The functor selecting the infinite cyclic group has a faithful nerve, since identifies with the underlying set of . It is not dense. Natural transformations between these nerves are arbitrary functions between their single underlying sets, since the source imposes no operations. A constant function with nonidentity value in a nontrivial target group is not induced by a homomorphism.  A dense functor need not be faithful. Let be the one-object category of a nontrivial small monoid, and send its object to a singleton and every arrow to the identity in . Its restricted nerve sends a set to the corresponding trivial right monoid action. Every function between two trivial actions is equivariant, so this nerve is fully faithful. The original functor identifies all monoid arrows.   "
