  "body": "  For an idempotent , the following conditions are equivalent:   The idempotent splits.  The pair has an equalizer.  The same pair has a coequalizer.   In a splitting, is that equalizer and that coequalizer. Both universal properties are preserved by every functor. Consequently, having all equalizers, or having all coequalizers, suffices for idempotent completeness.    Suppose and . If satisfies , then . The factorization is unique because is monic. Dually, if satisfies , then , uniquely because is epic.  Conversely, let equalize and . The equality gives a unique with . Since and is monic, . If instead coequalizes the pair, factor as ; the equation and epimorphy of give the same result. Every functor preserves the splitting equations, so the first paragraph establishes the transported universal properties as well.   "
