  "title": "Sets, orders, and one-object categories.",
  "body": " Sets, orders, and one-object categories   In , take , with inclusion and . This works for the empty set as well. The subset belongs to , so it proves idempotent completeness with the stipulated size convention.  A preorder regarded as a category has only identity endomorphisms, and is therefore idempotent complete. It need not have any initial or terminal object, or any binary products. Idempotent completeness is much weaker than finite completeness.  In the one-object category of a monoid , an idempotent splits precisely when there are with and . This is a condition within the monoid, not just the existence of the element . For the two-element monoid , with , the nonidentity idempotent does not split: a product is only when both factors are .   "
