  "number": "11.6",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  The functor is fully faithful. Every object of the envelope is a retract of an object in its image, and every idempotent in the envelope splits. Moreover, is an equivalence exactly when is idempotent complete.    Between and the arrow condition imposes no restriction, proving full faithfulness. The arrow , regarded once as and once in the reverse direction, exhibits the required retract: their composite on is its identity .  Now let be an idempotent endomorphism of . In it satisfies and . The object belongs to the envelope. The underlying arrow in both directions between and splits the given endomorphism, since the identity of is .  If splits in , its maps give an isomorphism . Thus idempotent completeness makes essentially surjective. Conversely, an isomorphism , read as two arrows of , supplies and . This proves the final equivalence.   "
