  "number": "14.9",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  For a category with pullbacks, the following are equivalent:   Every slice is cartesian closed.  For every arrow , pullback has a right adjoint .   Under these conditions, . For objects and in a slice, its exponential is     Suppose first that all slices are cartesian closed. Fix and . Work inside . The map is an arrow in from the object to the object . Exponentiating by gives an arrow . Let be the transpose of , and define by the pullback in  Such a pullback exists: every slice has its identity object as terminal object and inherits pullbacks, hence has finite limits.  For an object , a map is a map with . Transpose in the slice. The map becomes over , and the displayed equation becomes . This is exactly a map over , after the canonical interchange of the two pullback factors. The correspondence is natural in and , so it constructs the right adjoint and its arrow maps.  Conversely, suppose all dependent products exist. In , product with is the composite , up to its canonical product comparison. The two adjunctions and make this composite left adjoint to . Hence every product functor in the slice has a right adjoint, with the displayed exponential formula. Its terminal object is already , so the slice is cartesian closed.   "
